    "updated_at": "2026-11-22T19:26:44Z",
    "body": "Maintainer decision (question dialog, 2026-11-22), his option \"Targeted tests, heavy at the cut\", his words: \"Why are we doing heavy builds locally at all? should be small targeted shallow tests\".\n\n1. At review a PR needs only: its own pinning tests, the touched test binaries (the quick plan), the example catalog suites by name where a post or a toolpath changed, and the light lane (`cargo xtask gate light`).\n2. No full heavy lane for each PR. A diff that reaches a heavy lane is not \"not ready\" for that reason. No PR is \"owed heavy\".\n3. The full heavy lanes run only at the cut (the release tier on the cut runner) and on the post-merge and cut runners.\n4. A `P1` PR under the safety floor (wrong output) gets one targeted heavy step: the named heavy tests of the changed code. It never gets the whole lane. `P2` and lower get none.\n5. The heavy lock stays for the targeted step.\n6. `cargo xtask gate heavy --covers` stays as a command. It is not a condition for \"ready\" or for a merge.\n\nThe decision is the rule from now. The process files follow in one PR.\n\nThe process change is tracked in #3556.",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
